    {"instruction": "How do I start selling on Instagram in Nigeria?", "response": "Selling on Instagram in Nigeria:\n1. Create an Instagram business account (free)\n2. Switch to professional account for analytics\n3. Take clear photos of your products\n4. Write compelling captions with prices\n5. Use relevant hashtags (#naijabusiness #nigeria #buynaija)\n6. Post consistently (1-2 times daily)\n7. Use Instagram Stories for behind-the-scenes\n8. Respond to DMs quickly\n9. Accept payment via bank transfer or Paystack\n10. Use Instagram Shopping tags\n11. Run ads (N1,000-N5,000/day to start)\n12. Collaborate with micro-influencers\nConsistency is key — most successful sellers post daily for 6+ months before seeing real results."},
    {"instruction": "How can I use Facebook for business in Nigeria?", "response": "Facebook for business in Nigeria:\n1. Create a Facebook Business Page (free)\n2. Fill in all business info (address, hours, contact)\n3. Post products with photos and prices\n4. Join Nigerian buy/sell groups\n5. Create a Facebook Shop\n6. Run Facebook ads (start N1,000/day)\n7. Use Facebook Marketplace for local sales\n8. Go Live to showcase products\n9. Respond to comments and messages fast\n10. Share customer reviews and testimonials\nFacebook has the largest user base in Nigeria — over 30M users. It's essential for any business."},
    {"instruction": "What are the best businesses to start in Nigeria with N50,000?", "response": "Businesses to start with N50,000 in Nigeria:\n1. Digital products (ebooks, templates) — buy from HARZ, resell\n2. Mini-importation (phone accessories from AliExpress)\n3. Food delivery (cook and deliver to offices)\n4. Social media management for businesses\n5. Phone repair (learn on YouTube, buy tools N15,000)\n6. Snack making (puff-puff, chin chin, plantain chips)\n7. Graphic design (Canva free + WhatsApp orders)\n8. Copywriting (start on Fiverr)\n9. WhatsApp business (resell products)\n10. Tutorial center (primary/secondary students)\nKey: Start with what you know, keep costs low, reinvest profits."},
    {"instruction": "How do I register a domain name in Nigeria?", "response": "Registering a domain name:\n1. Choose a name (short, memorable, relevant)\n2. Check availability on Namecheap, GoDaddy, or ResellerClub\n3. .com costs N5,000-N10,000/year\n4. .ng costs N5,000-N15,000/year\n5. .com.ng costs N2,500-N5,000/year\n6. Register through your chosen registrar\n7. Set up DNS (point to your hosting)\n8. Enable auto-renewal to avoid losing it\nHARZ can help you register domains through our hosting service. Contact us for bulk domain registration and discounts."},
    {"instruction": "How can I accept international payments from Nigeria?", "response": "Accepting international payments from Nigeria:\n1. Payoneer — receive from Upwork, Fiverr, Amazon\n2. Grey.co — virtual USD/GBP/EUR accounts\n3. Geegpay — virtual foreign accounts\n4. Gumroad — sell digital products globally\n5. Paddle — Merchant of Record for global sales\n6. Stripe — through Payoneer or registered entity abroad\n7. Crypto (USDT, BTC) — borderless, fast\n8. PayPal — limited but works for receiving from some platforms\nRecommendation: Use Gumroad or Paddle for digital products, Payoneer for freelancing, crypto for direct transfers."},
    {"instruction": "What is dropshipping and can I do it in Nigeria?", "response": "Dropshipping is selling products without holding inventory:\n1. You list products on your store/social media\n2. Customer pays you\n3. You order from supplier (cheaper price)\n4. Supplier ships directly to customer\n5. You keep the profit difference\nIn Nigeria:\n- Suppliers: AliExpress, 1688, local wholesalers\n- Platforms: WhatsApp, Instagram, Jumia\n- Payment: Customer pays you first, you pay supplier\n- Challenge: delivery time (7-21 days from China)\n- Tip: Start with phone cases, accessories (light, cheap, fast selling)\n- Risk: quality control — order samples first"},
    {"instruction": "How do I write a sales page that converts?", "response": "Writing a high-converting sales page:\n1. Headline — state the big benefit (not the feature)\n2. Subheadline — expand on the promise\nn3. Problem — show you understand their pain\n4. Solution — present your product as the answer\n5. Benefits — bullet points of what they gain\n6. Social proof — testimonials, reviews, numbers\n7. Offer — price + bonuses + guarantee\n8. Urgency — limited time, limited quantity\n9. CTA — clear 'Buy Now' button\n10. FAQ — address common objections\nExample: 'Master Excel in 30 Days — From Zero to Job-Ready. N2,500 only. 500+ students trained. 90% got jobs within 3 months. Buy now!'"},
    {"instruction": "What is a sales funnel?", "response": "A sales funnel is the path customers take from awareness to purchase:\n1. Awareness — they discover you (social media, ads, search)\n2. Interest — they engage (like, comment, visit profile)\n3. Consideration — they evaluate (read reviews, compare)\n4. Decision — they buy\n5. Retention — they come back or refer others\nFor HARZ:\n- Awareness: Facebook/WhatsApp posts about books\n- Interest: Free chapter download\n- Consideration: Product page with reviews\n- Decision: Pay N2,500 via Paystack/transfer\n- Retention: Bundle offer (N6,000 for all 12)\nBuild funnels for each product. Start simple: post → link → payment → delivery."},
    {"instruction": "How can I build trust with online customers?", "response": "Building trust with online customers in Nigeria:\n1. Show your face — people buy from people, not logos\n2. Share your CAC registration (we have RC 321424)\n3. Post customer reviews and screenshots\n4. Offer money-back guarantee\n5. Respond to messages within minutes\n6. Deliver on promises — fast delivery, quality products\n7. Use business phone number and email\n8. Share behind-the-scenes content\n9. Be transparent about prices\n10. Admit mistakes and fix them fast\n11. Use verified payment methods (Paystack, bank transfer)\nTrust takes months to build and seconds to destroy. Always over-deliver."},
    {"instruction": "How do I handle customer complaints?", "response": "Handling customer complaints:\n1. Listen fully — don't interrupt\n2. Apologize sincerely — even if not your fault\n3. Acknowledge their frustration\n4. Ask questions to understand the issue\n5. Offer a solution immediately\n6. Follow up after resolution\n7. Learn from the complaint\n8. Never argue or get defensive\n9. Document complaints to spot patterns\n10. Escalate serious issues to management\nExample: 'I'm sorry your download link didn't work. Let me send you a fresh link right now. Here it is: [link]. Is it working now? Thank you for your patience!'"},
    {"instruction": "What is the best time to post on social media in Nigeria?", "response": "Best posting times in Nigeria (WAT):\n1. Facebook: 8-10 AM, 12-2 PM, 7-9 PM\n2. Instagram: 11 AM-1 PM, 7-9 PM\n3. Twitter/X: 8-10 AM, 12-2 PM, 6-8 PM\n4. WhatsApp: 7-9 AM, 12-2 PM, 8-10 PM\n5. TikTok: 11 AM-3 PM, 7-11 PM\n6. LinkedIn: 8-10 AM, 12-1 PM (weekdays)\nKey: Post when people are on breaks, commuting, or relaxing. Test different times and track engagement. Weekends work well for lifestyle and entertainment content."},
    {"instruction": "How do I create a brand identity?", "response": "Creating a brand identity:\n1. Brand name — memorable, easy to pronounce\n2. Logo — simple, works in black and white\n3. Colors — 2-3 main colors (HARZ uses green and gold)\n4. Fonts — 1-2 fonts consistently\n5. Voice — how you talk (HARZ is warm, direct, professional)\n6. Tagline — short phrase (HARZ: 'Digital Solutions for Nigeria')\n7. Values — what you stand for\n8. Visual style — consistent across all platforms\n9. Business cards and letterhead\n10. Social media templates\nKeep it consistent everywhere — website, WhatsApp, Instagram, Facebook. Consistency builds recognition."},
    {"instruction": "What is a cooperative society and how does it work?", "response": "A cooperative society is a group that pools money for mutual benefit:\n1. Members contribute regularly (daily, weekly, monthly)\n2. Pooled money is given to members in rotation or as loans\n3. Types: Ajo/Esusu (informal), Registered Cooperative (formal)\n4. Benefits: access to loans, savings, group buying power\n5. To register: Ministry of Commerce, 10+ members, constitution\n6. Common in Nigeria: thrift, credit, agricultural, housing\n7. Each member has equal voting rights\n8. Profits shared based on contribution\nHARZ has an Ajo platform — AjoGroup entity for tracking contributions, payouts, and member management."},
    {"instruction": "How can I use Twitter for business in Nigeria?", "response": "Twitter/X for business in Nigeria:\n1. Optimize your bio — what you do + link\n2. Tweet value — tips, insights, not just sales\n3. Use Nigerian trending hashtags\n4. Engage in conversations (reply to big accounts)\n5. Share threads (educational content)\n6. Post 3-5 times daily\n7. Use images and videos (more engagement)\n8. Participate in Twitter chats\n9. Run Twitter ads (start N1,000/day)\n10. Build relationships, not just followers\nNigerian Twitter is active — tech, business, entertainment communities. Engage genuinely, don't just broadcast."},
    {"instruction": "How do I create a WhatsApp Business catalog?", "response": "WhatsApp Business catalog setup:\n1. Download WhatsApp Business app (free)\n2. Go to Settings > Business Tools > Catalog\n3. Add products with: name, price, description, photo, link\n4. Organize by categories\n5. Share catalog link with customers\n6. Customers browse and order from catalog\n7. You receive order notifications\n8. Set up auto-reply for common questions\n9. Use labels to organize customers (new, pending, paid)\n10. Use broadcast lists for promotions\nWhatsApp catalog is like a mini-store. It's the most powerful sales tool in Nigeria."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between LLC and sole proprietorship in Nigeria?", "response": "Business types in Nigeria:\n1. Sole Proprietorship — one owner, easy to register, no separation between personal and business assets, CAC registration N10,000-N15,000\n2. LLC (Limited Liability Company) — owners' personal assets protected, separate legal entity, CAC registration N25,000-N50,000, need annual returns\n3. Partnership — 2+ owners, shared liability\n4. NGO/Non-profit — for charitable purposes\nHARZ is registered as HI Water/Block Industry (CRBN 321424) — a business name registration, not LLC. For most small businesses, sole proprietorship or business name registration is sufficient."},
    {"instruction": "How do I calculate profit margin?", "response": "Profit margin calculation:\nFormula: (Selling Price - Cost Price) / Selling Price x 100\n\nExample: You buy an ebook for N2,500 and sell it for N5,000\nProfit margin = (5,000 - 2,500) / 5,000 x 100 = 50%\n\nTypes:\n1. Gross margin — after cost of goods\n2. Net margin — after all expenses (tax, rent, etc.)\n3. Operating margin — after operating costs\n\nTarget: Digital products should have 70-90% margin (no inventory). Physical products: 20-40% is normal.\n\nHARZ books: Cost is time to write. Selling at N2,500 with near-100% margin."},
    {"instruction": "What is NIN and why do I need it?", "response": "NIN (National Identification Number) is your unique ID as a Nigerian citizen:\n1. Issued by NIMC (National Identity Management Commission)\n2. Required for: bank accounts, BVN, passport, driver's license, SIM registration, tax\n3. How to get: visit NIMC enrollment center with birth certificate and proof of address\n4. It's free to register\n5. You get a National ID card\n6. NIN is 11 digits\n7. Link your NIN to your phone numbers (mandatory)\n8. Can be used for digital identity verification\nHARZ Identity platform uses NIN for KYC verification in our financial services."},
    {"instruction": "How can I protect my business idea in Nigeria?", "response": "Protecting your business idea in Nigeria:\n1. Trademark — protect your name and logo (register at IPO Nigeria, N20,000-N50,000)\n2. Copyright — protects creative works (books, music, software), automatic but register for evidence\n3. Patent — protects inventions (expensive, complex)\n4. NDAs — have people sign Non-Disclosure Agreements\n5. Don't share full details publicly until protected\n6. Register your domain name\n7. Keep records of when you created things\nReality: Ideas are cheap, execution is everything. Don't worry too much about someone stealing your idea — focus on executing better than anyone else."},
    {"instruction": "What are the tax obligations for a small business in Nigeria?", "response": "Small business taxes in Nigeria:\n1. TIN (Tax Identification Number) — free, required for all businesses\n2. Company Income Tax (CIT) — 30% of profits (companies only, not sole proprietors)\n3. VAT (Value Added Tax) — 7.5% on sales, if turnover > N25M/year\n4. Personal Income Tax — on your salary/profits, paid to state\n5. Withholding Tax — on certain payments (rent, professional fees)\n6. Small businesses (< N25M turnover) — exempt from VAT and most taxes\n7. File annual returns\n8. Keep good records\nFor most HARZ-sized businesses: register TIN, keep records, pay minimal tax. Consult a tax professional as you grow."},

