    # ========== TECH & PROGRAMMING DEEP DIVE (15) ==========
    {"instruction": "What is JavaScript?", "response": "JavaScript is the programming language of the web:\n1. Runs in every browser (Chrome, Firefox, Safari)\n2. Makes websites interactive (buttons, forms, animations)\n3. Used with HTML (structure) and CSS (styling)\n4. Can also run on servers (Node.js)\n5. Used to build: web apps, mobile apps, games, bots\n6. Easy to learn, hard to master\n7. Huge job market — every web developer needs it\n8. Frameworks: React, Vue, Angular\nOur Web Development book (N2,500) teaches JavaScript fundamentals including DOM manipulation and form validation."},
    {"instruction": "What is the difference between frontend and backend?", "response": "Frontend vs Backend:\n\nFrontend (what users see):\n- HTML, CSS, JavaScript\n- Buttons, forms, layouts, animations\n- Frameworks: React, Vue, Angular\n- Focus: user experience\n\nBackend (what users don't see):\n- Servers, databases, APIs\n- Python, Node.js, PHP, Java\n- Data storage, authentication, business logic\n- Frameworks: Django, Express, Laravel\n\nFull-stack = does both\n\nHARZ: Our frontend is HTML/CSS/JS (GitHub Pages). Backend is Node.js (Render) + Base44 (entities/APIs)."},
    {"instruction": "How do I deploy a website for free?", "response": "Free website hosting options:\n1. GitHub Pages — static sites (HTML/CSS/JS), free forever, custom domain support\n2. Vercel — Next.js, React, static sites, free tier\n3. Netlify — static sites, free tier\n4. Render — web services, free tier (sleeps after inactivity)\n5. Cloudflare Pages — static sites, free, fast CDN\n6. Firebase Hosting — static + serverless, free tier\n7. HuggingFace Spaces — AI apps, free\nHARZ uses GitHub Pages for all 21+ sites and Render for backend services. Total cost: N0."},
    {"instruction": "What is an API and how do I use one?", "response": "API (Application Programming Interface) lets programs talk to each other:\n\nExample using HARZ Paystack API:\n```\nfetch('https://api.paystack.co/transaction/initialize', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer sk_xxx' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({\n    email: 'customer@email.com',\n    amount: 250000  // N2,500 in kobo\n  })\n})\n```\n\nThis sends a request to Paystack to create a payment link. The API returns a link you give to your customer.\n\nAPIs are how HARZ connects to: Paystack, Gumroad, OpenRouter (AI), GitHub, Render, Telegram, WhatsApp."},
    {"instruction": "How do I learn data analysis?", "response": "Learning data analysis:\n1. Start with Excel — most data analysis tool in the world\n2. Learn PivotTables, VLOOKUP, charts\n3. Learn SQL — query databases\n4. Learn Python (pandas library) — advanced analysis\n5. Learn visualization — Tableau, Power BI\n6. Practice with real datasets (Kaggle.com)\n7. Build a portfolio of analysis projects\n8. Key skills: statistics, data cleaning, visualization, storytelling\nOur Data Analysis & Excel Analytics book (N2,500) covers statistics, forecasting, Power Pivot, and DAX."},
    {"instruction": "What is machine learning?", "response": "Machine learning is teaching computers to learn from data:\n1. Supervised learning — learn from labeled examples (spam detection)\n2. Unsupervised learning — find patterns in data (customer segmentation)\n3. Reinforcement learning — learn by trial and error (game AI)\n\nSteps:\n1. Collect data\n2. Clean and prepare data\n3. Choose a model (algorithm)\n4. Train the model on data\n5. Test the model on new data\n6. Deploy and use\n\nTools: Python, TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn\n\nHARZ AI itself is a fine-tuned machine learning model — trained on HARZ business data."},
    {"instruction": "What is Git and GitHub?", "response": "Git is version control — tracks changes to your code:\n1. Like 'save' but for entire projects\n2. Track who changed what and when\n3. Go back to any previous version\n4. Work with teams without conflicts\n\nGitHub is a website that hosts Git repositories:\n1. Store your code online\n2. Share with others\n3. Collaborate on projects\n4. Free for public repos\n5. GitHub Pages = free website hosting\n\nHARZ has 61+ repos on GitHub (github.com/rabiuhamza11) — all our platforms, books, and tools."},
    {"instruction": "What is responsive web design?", "response": "Responsive web design makes websites work on all screen sizes:\n1. Mobile (320-480px) — most Nigerian users\n2. Tablet (768-1024px)\n3. Desktop (1024px+)\n\nTechniques:\n1. CSS Media Queries — different styles for different screens\n2. Flexbox — flexible layouts\n3. CSS Grid — 2D layouts\n4. Mobile-first approach — design for mobile, then scale up\n5. Responsive images — scale with screen\n6. Touch-friendly buttons (min 44px)\n\nHARZ websites are optimized for Infinix Hot 10i (2GB RAM) — minimal JavaScript, fast loading, mobile-first."},
    {"instruction": "How do I make my website load faster?", "response": "Website speed optimization:\n1. Compress images — use WebP, keep under 100KB\n2. Minify CSS/JS — remove whitespace\n3. Use CDN — Cloudflare (free)\n4. Enable caching — browser stores files\n5. Fewer HTTP requests — combine files\n6. Lazy load images — load on scroll\n7. Use Gzip compression\n8. Minimal JavaScript — only what's needed\n9. Fast hosting — GitHub Pages, Vercel\n10. Test: Google PageSpeed Insights, GTmetrix\n\nHARZ target: All pages under 50KB for 2GB RAM devices. Our lite app is only 9KB!"},
    {"instruction": "What is CSS and why is it important?", "response": "CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how websites look:\n1. Colors, fonts, spacing, layout\n2. Responsive design — adapts to screen size\n3. Animations and transitions\n4. Without CSS, websites look like plain text documents\n5. Easy to learn — simpler than programming\n6. Works with HTML (structure) and JavaScript (behavior)\n\nExample: `body { background: #0a0e17; color: white; font-family: sans-serif; }`\n\nOur Web Development book (N2,500) teaches CSS including Flexbox, Grid, and responsive design."},
    {"instruction": "What is a web hosting?", "response": "Web hosting is where your website files live:\n1. Shared hosting — multiple sites on one server (Namecheap, N1,500-N5,000/month)\n2. VPS — your own virtual server (N5,000-N25,000/month)\n3. Cloud hosting — scalable, pay as you go (AWS, Render, Vercel)\n4. Free hosting — GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel (with limits)\n5. Dedicated — entire server for you (N50,000+/month)\n\nHARZ uses: GitHub Pages (free, 21+ sites) + Render (free tier, backend) + Base44 (entities/APIs). Total: N0/month."},
    {"instruction": "How does Paystack work?", "response": "Paystack is a Nigerian payment gateway:\n1. Customer clicks 'Pay' on your website\n2. Paystack opens a secure payment form\n3. Customer enters card details (or bank transfer, USSD)\n4. Paystack processes payment securely\n5. Money goes to your bank account (1-2 days)\n6. Paystack sends webhook notification to your server\n7. You deliver the product\n\nFees: 1.5% local cards (cap N2,000), 3.9% international\n\nHARZ uses Paystack for live card payments. All 10 books have Paystack checkout links at N3,200 each."},
    {"instruction": "What is a webhook?", "response": "A webhook is an automated message sent from one app to another:\n1. Like a phone call — 'Hey, this happened!'\n2. When event occurs, app A sends HTTP POST to app B\n3. App B receives and processes it\n\nExamples in HARZ:\n- Paystack webhook: 'Payment successful for order #123'\n- NOWPayments webhook: 'USDT payment confirmed'\n- Paddle webhook: 'New subscription created'\n- GitHub webhook: 'New push to repo'\n\nOur backend receives webhooks and automatically delivers products, updates orders, and sends notifications."},
    {"instruction": "What is cloud storage?", "response": "Cloud storage is saving files on remote servers instead of your device:\n1. Access files from anywhere with internet\n2. Don't lose files if device breaks\n3. Share files easily\n4. Automatic sync across devices\n\nOptions:\n1. Google Drive — 15GB free\n2. Dropbox — 2GB free\n3. OneDrive — 5GB free\n4. GitHub — code and small files\n5. HARZ File Storage — CDN-backed, secure\n\nHARZ stores all book PDFs on GitHub Pages with CDN delivery. Every book has a direct download link."},
    {"instruction": "How do I secure my website?", "response": "Website security checklist:\n1. Use HTTPS (SSL certificate) — free with Let's Encrypt\n2. Strong passwords for admin accounts\n3. Keep software and plugins updated\n4. Use 2FA for admin login\n5. Regular backups\n6. Input validation — prevent SQL injection\n7. Use CSRF tokens for forms\n8. Rate limiting — prevent brute force\n9. Security headers (X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options)\n10. Regular security scans\nHARZ Cloud has RBAC (6 roles), RLS, 2FA, and WAF for security."},

    # ========== HEALTH & WELLNESS DEEP DIVE (15) ==========
    {"instruction": "What are the symptoms of malaria?", "response": "Malaria symptoms:\n1. Fever (often high, 38°C+)\n2. Chills and sweating\n3. Headache\n4. Body and joint pain\n5. Nausea and vomiting\n6. Fatigue\n7. Loss of appetite\n8. Diarrhea\n\nIf you suspect malaria, do a blood test immediately. Treatment is available and effective if caught early. Use mosquito nets, repellents, and clear stagnant water around your home.\n\nNote: I'm an AI, not a doctor. Always consult a healthcare professional for medical advice."},
    {"instruction": "How can I boost my immune system naturally?", "response": "Natural immune boosters:\n1. Eat fruits rich in vitamin C — oranges, lemons, guava\n2. Get enough sleep — 7-8 hours\n3. Exercise regularly — 30 min daily\n4. Manage stress — pray, meditate, talk to friends\n5. Drink plenty water — 2-3 liters\n6. Eat garlic and ginger — natural antivirals\n7. Get sunlight — vitamin D\n8. Eat vegetables — ugu, spinach, broccoli\n9. Limit sugar — it weakens immunity\n10. Don't smoke, limit alcohol\n11. Wash hands regularly\n12. Take zinc — nuts, seeds, beans"},
    {"instruction": "What should I do if I have a fever?", "response": "Managing a fever at home:\n1. Rest — don't overexert yourself\n2. Drink plenty of fluids — water, soup, juice\n3. Use a damp cloth on forehead\n4. Wear light clothing\n5. Take paracetamol (not aspirin for children)\n6. Monitor temperature regularly\n7. Eat light — soup, fruits\n8. Stay in a well-ventilated room\n\nSee a doctor if:\n- Temperature above 39°C\n- Fever lasts more than 3 days\n- Severe headache or stiff neck\n- Difficulty breathing\n- Confusion or drowsiness\n- In a child under 5\n\nNote: I'm an AI, not a doctor. Seek medical attention for serious symptoms."},
    {"instruction": "How can I eat healthy on a budget in Nigeria?", "response": "Healthy eating on a budget in Nigeria:\n1. Buy seasonal fruits — they're cheaper\n2. Eat more beans and eggs — cheap protein\n3. Buy at local markets (not supermarkets)\n4. Cook at home — avoid buying food outside\n5. Buy in bulk — rice, beans, garri\n6. Eat more vegetables — ugu, okra, garden eggs\n7. Drink water instead of soda\n8. Eat fruits for snacks instead of chips\n9. Prepare meals in batches\n10. Grow some vegetables at home\nBudget meal: Beans + rice + vegetable soup = N500 per meal, balanced and filling."},
    {"instruction": "What are common mental health challenges in Nigeria?", "response": "Common mental health challenges in Nigeria:\n1. Depression — persistent sadness, loss of interest\n2. Anxiety — excessive worry, panic attacks\n3. Stress — from work, finances, relationships\n4. Substance abuse — alcohol, drugs\n5. PTSD — from trauma, conflict\n6. Sleep disorders — insomnia\n\nStigma is the biggest barrier. If you're struggling:\n1. Talk to someone you trust\n2. Seek professional help (it's not weakness)\n3. Practice self-care — rest, exercise, eat well\n4. Join support groups\n5. Avoid isolation\n6. Call a helpline if in crisis\n\nHARZ has MindCare platform for mental wellness tracking, journaling, and therapy exercises. You're not alone."},
    {"instruction": "How can I improve my mental health?", "response": "Improving mental health:\n1. Talk about your feelings — don't bottle them up\n2. Stay connected — friends, family, community\n3. Exercise regularly — releases endorphins\n4. Sleep well — 7-8 hours\n5. Eat nutritious food\n6. Limit alcohol and avoid drugs\n7. Practice gratitude — write 3 good things daily\n8. Take breaks — rest is productive\n9. Set boundaries — learn to say no\n10. Seek help when needed — professional counseling\n11. Pray or meditate\n12. Spend time in nature\nHARZ MindCare platform offers mood tracking, journaling, meditation, and therapy exercises. Mental health is health."},
    {"instruction": "What are the benefits of exercise?", "response": "Benefits of regular exercise:\n1. Physical: stronger muscles, better heart, weight management\n2. Mental: reduces stress, anxiety, depression\n3. Energy: more stamina throughout the day\n4. Sleep: better quality sleep\n5. Brain: improved memory and focus\n6. Immunity: stronger immune system\n7. Confidence: better self-esteem\n8. Longevity: live longer, healthier\n9. Disease prevention: reduces risk of diabetes, hypertension\n10. Mood: releases endorphins (feel-good hormones)\n\nStart small: 15-minute walk daily. Gradually increase. Consistency beats intensity. Our OMEGA Health AI platform helps track fitness plans."},
    {"instruction": "What is hypertension and how do I prevent it?", "response": "Hypertension (high blood pressure):\n\nNormal: 120/80 or below\nHigh: 140/90 or above\n\nPrevention:\n1. Reduce salt intake\n2. Exercise regularly\n3. Maintain healthy weight\n4. Limit alcohol\n5. Don't smoke\n6. Manage stress\n7. Eat more fruits and vegetables\n8. Check blood pressure regularly (pharmacies offer free checks)\n9. Limit caffeine\n10. Get enough sleep\n\nMany Nigerians have hypertension without knowing. It's called the 'silent killer.' Get checked today — it's free at most pharmacies."},
    {"instruction": "How can I quit smoking?", "response": "Quitting smoking:\n1. Set a quit date — commit to it\n2. Tell family and friends for support\n3. Identify triggers — when do you smoke?\n4. Replace with healthy habits — walk, chew gum, water\n5. Avoid smoking environments and friends who smoke\n6. Use nicotine replacement (patches, gum) if needed\n7. Download a quit-smoking app for daily tracking\n8. Reward yourself for milestones (1 day, 1 week, 1 month)\n9. Don't give up if you relapse — try again\n10. See a doctor for medical support\n\nBenefits start immediately: 20 min after quitting, heart rate drops. 1 year: heart disease risk halves."},
    {"instruction": "What are healthy Nigerian foods?", "response": "Healthy Nigerian foods:\n\nProtein: beans, eggs, fish, chicken, turkey, moi-moi, akara\nVegetables: ugu (pumpkin leaves), spinach, okra, garden egg, bitter leaf, waterleaf\nCarbs: sweet potato, yam (moderate), brown rice, whole wheat, oats\nFruits: oranges, bananas, watermelon, pawpaw, mango, guava\nHealthy fats: avocado, groundnuts, palm oil (moderate)\nHealthy drinks: water, zobo (no sugar), kunu, fresh juice\n\nAvoid: excess fried food, too much salt, sugary drinks, processed foods, excessive stock cubes\n\nNigerian food can be very healthy — it's about how you prepare it. Steam and boil more, fry less."},
    {"instruction": "How much sleep do I need?", "response": "Sleep needs by age:\n1. Newborns (0-3 months): 14-17 hours\n2. Infants (4-12 months): 12-16 hours\n3. Children (1-5 years): 10-14 hours\n4. Children (6-12 years): 9-12 hours\n5. Teenagers (13-18): 8-10 hours\n6. Adults (18-64): 7-9 hours\n7. Older adults (65+): 7-8 hours\n\nSigns you need more sleep: fatigue, poor concentration, mood swings, weight gain, weak immunity. Quality matters as much as quantity — dark room, no screens before bed, comfortable temperature."},
    {"instruction": "What are the warning signs of a stroke?", "response": "Stroke warning signs (remember FAST):\nF — Face drooping on one side\nA — Arm weakness (can't raise both arms)\nS — Speech difficulty (slurred or confused)\nT — Time to call for help immediately\n\nOther signs:\n1. Sudden numbness on one side\n2. Sudden severe headache\n3. Vision problems\n4. Dizziness, loss of balance\n5. Confusion\n\nIf you see these signs, go to the hospital IMMEDIATELY. Every minute counts. Don't wait to see if it passes.\n\nNote: I'm an AI. For medical emergencies, call for help or go to the nearest hospital."},
    {"instruction": "How can I manage diabetes?", "response": "Managing diabetes:\n1. Monitor blood sugar regularly\n2. Take medication as prescribed\n3. Eat balanced meals — low sugar, high fiber\n4. Exercise daily — 30 min walk\n5. Maintain healthy weight\n6. Limit refined carbs (white rice, bread, sugar)\n7. Eat more vegetables and protein\n8. Stay hydrated\n9. Get regular checkups (eyes, feet, kidneys)\n10. Avoid smoking and excess alcohol\n11. Manage stress\n12. Get enough sleep\n\nNigerian diet tips: Replace white rice with brown, eat more beans, reduce sugary drinks ( minerals, zobo with sugar)."},
    {"instruction": "What is first aid for burns?", "response": "First aid for minor burns:\n1. Cool the burn under running water for 10-20 minutes\n2. Don't use ice — it damages skin\n3. Cover with clean, non-stick cloth\n4. Don't apply toothpaste, butter, or oil (common Nigerian myth — don't do it!)\n5. Take paracetamol for pain\n6. Don't pop blisters\n7. Keep the area clean\n\nFor severe burns (large area, face, hands, genitals):\n1. Call for emergency help immediately\n2. Don't remove stuck clothing\n3. Cover with clean cloth\n4. Don't apply anything to the burn\n5. Keep the person warm and calm\n\nNote: For serious burns, seek medical attention immediately."},
    {"instruction": "How can I stay hydrated?", "response": "Staying hydrated:\n1. Drink 2-3 liters (8-12 cups) of water daily\n2. Drink more in hot weather or when exercising\n3. Carry a water bottle everywhere\n4. Drink water first thing in the morning\n5. Eat water-rich fruits — watermelon, oranges, cucumber\n6. Drink before you're thirsty — thirst means you're already dehydrated\n7. Limit caffeine and alcohol (they dehydrate)\n8. Infuse water with lemon or cucumber for taste\n\nSigns of dehydration: dark urine, headache, fatigue, dizziness, dry mouth, confusion.\nIn Nigerian heat, dehydration happens fast — always carry water."},

    # ========== EDUCATION DEEP DIVE (10) ==========
    {"instruction": "How can I learn to code for free?", "response": "Free coding resources:\n1. freeCodeCamp — free courses, certificates (HTML, CSS, JS, Python)\n2. W3Schools — tutorials and references (all languages)\n3. YouTube — CS50, Programming with Mosh, freeCodeCamp channel\n4. Codecademy — free tier for basics\n5. MDN Web Docs — web development reference\n6. Khan Academy — computer science basics\n7. SoloLearn — mobile app, learn on the go\n8. The Odin Project — full-stack curriculum\n9. HARZ Python Programming book — N2,500 (comprehensive, practical)\n10. HARZ Web Development book — N2,500\n\nStart with HTML/CSS, then JavaScript, then Python. Practice daily. Build projects. Don't just watch — CODE."},
    {"instruction": "What jobs can I get with digital skills in Nigeria?", "response": "Digital skills jobs in Nigeria:\n1. Web developer — N150,000-N500,000/month\n2. Digital marketer — N100,000-N400,000/month\n3. Data analyst — N200,000-N600,000/month\n4. Graphic designer — N80,000-N300,000/month\n5. Social media manager — N80,000-N250,000/month\n6. Copywriter — N100,000-N500,000/month (freelance)\n7. Python developer — N200,000-N800,000/month\n8. UI/UX designer — N150,000-N500,000/month\n9. Cybersecurity analyst — N200,000-N1,000,000/month\n10. Content creator — N50,000-N2,000,000/month\nMost of these can be done remotely for international clients at higher rates."},
    {"instruction": "How do I prepare for a job interview?", "response": "Job interview preparation:\n1. Research the company — what they do, mission, values\n2. Review the job description carefully\n3. Prepare your CV and print copies\n4. Dress professionally (Nigerian business attire)\n5. Prepare answers for common questions:\n   - 'Tell me about yourself'\n   - 'Why do you want this job?'\n   - 'What are your strengths and weaknesses?'\n   - 'Where do you see yourself in 5 years?'\n6. Prepare questions to ask them\n7. Practice with a friend or mirror\n8. Arrive 15 minutes early\n9. Be confident but humble\n10. Follow up with a thank you message"},
    {"instruction": "How do I write a CV?", "response": "Writing a professional CV:\n1. Contact info — name, phone, email, location\n2. Professional summary — 2-3 lines about you\n3. Work experience — company, role, dates, achievements (not just duties)\n4. Education — school, degree, year\n5. Skills — relevant to the job\n6. Certifications — if any\n7. Keep it to 1-2 pages\n8. Use professional font (Arial, Calibri)\n9. No photos (unless requested)\n10. Save as PDF\n11. Use action verbs: 'managed', 'created', 'increased'\n12. Include numbers: 'increased sales by 30%'\n\nTip: Tailor your CV for each job. Use keywords from the job description."},
    {"instruction": "What is the importance of continuous learning?", "response": "Continuous learning is essential because:\n1. Technology changes fast — skills become obsolete\n2. Career growth requires new knowledge\n3. Income increases with skills\n4. Keeps your brain sharp\n5. Opens new opportunities\n6. Builds confidence\n7. Prevents stagnation\n8. Makes you adaptable\n9. Sets you apart from others\n10. The world rewards those who keep learning\n\nIn Nigeria, the gap between those who learn and those who don't is growing fast. Digital skills especially — they're the fastest path to better income. HARZ books are designed for continuous, practical learning."},
    {"instruction": "How can I study for exams effectively?", "response": "Effective exam preparation:\n1. Start early — don't cram\n2. Create a study schedule\n3. Break topics into manageable chunks\n4. Use active recall — test yourself\n5. Practice with past questions\n6. Study in 25-minute sessions (Pomodoro)\n7. Take 5-minute breaks between sessions\n8. Teach others what you learn\n9. Use visual aids — diagrams, mind maps\n10. Get enough sleep before the exam\n11. Eat well and exercise\n12. Find a quiet study space\n13. Turn off phone notifications\n14. Review notes within 24 hours of learning\n15. Focus on understanding, not memorizing"},
    {"instruction": "What is critical thinking and why is it important?", "response": "Critical thinking is analyzing information objectively:\n1. Question everything — don't just accept\n2. Look for evidence, not assumptions\n3. Consider multiple perspectives\n4. Identify biases (yours and others)\n5. Evaluate arguments logically\n6. Make informed decisions\n7. Don't follow the crowd blindly\n\nWhy it matters:\n- Helps you make better decisions\n- Protects you from scams and misinformation\n- Improves problem-solving\n- Makes you a better employee/entrepreneur\n- Essential in the age of social media misinformation\n\nPractice: When you see a claim, ask: 'What's the evidence? Who benefits? What's the alternative explanation?'"},
    {"instruction": "How do I improve my English speaking?", "response": "Improving English speaking:\n1. Read aloud daily — 10 minutes\n2. Talk to yourself in English (in private)\n3. Watch English movies with subtitles\n4. Listen to English podcasts and news\n5. Practice with a language partner\n6. Record yourself and listen\n7. Learn pronunciation rules\n8. Learn 5 new words daily and use them\n9. Think in English, not your native language\n10. Don't fear mistakes — correct and move on\n11. Join English-speaking groups or clubs\n12. Use apps: Duolingo, Hello English\n\nNigerian English is fine — you don't need an American/British accent. Focus on clear communication, not accent."},
    {"instruction": "What are the benefits of reading books?", "response": "Benefits of reading:\n1. Improves vocabulary and language\n2. Enhances focus and concentration\n3. Reduces stress (reading is relaxing)\n4. Improves memory\n5. Builds knowledge\n6. Develops critical thinking\n7. Improves writing skills\n8. Increases empathy and understanding\n9. Better sleep (reading before bed)\n10. Low cost entertainment\n11. Can earn you money (apply what you learn)\n12. Boosts creativity\n\nHARZ Digital Learning books are designed to be practical — you don't just read, you learn skills that increase your income. N2,500 invested can return N500,000+ in new income."},
    {"instruction": "How can I learn faster?", "response": "Learning faster and better:\n1. Use the Feynman Technique — explain it simply\n2. Active recall — test yourself, don't just re-read\n3. Spaced repetition — review at increasing intervals\n4. Interleaving — mix different topics\n5. Use multiple senses — read, write, speak, draw\n6. Connect new knowledge to what you know\n7. Practice immediately — don't just study\n8. Teach someone else\n9. Use mnemonics and memory tricks\n10. Sleep well — brain consolidates learning during sleep\n11. Exercise — improves brain function\n12. Remove distractions — phone off, clean space"},

    # ========== HAUSA DEEP DIVE (15) ==========
    {"instruction": "Yadda zan fara kasuwanci a intanet?", "response": "Don fara kasuwanci a intanet:\n1. Kayi tunani akan abin da za ka siyar\n2. Ka buɗe asusun WhatsApp Business (kyauta)\n3. Ka saka hotunan products dinka\n4. Ka rubuta farashi a bayyane\n5. Ka yi amfani da Instagram da Facebook\n6. Ka karɓi biyan kuɗi ta bank transfer ko Paystack\n7. Ka amsa saƙon nan da nan\n8. Ka saka customers a cikin broadcast list\n9. Ka fara karami, ka sake saka riba\n10. Ka ci gaba da tura posts kullum"},
    {"instruction": "Me ne crypto?", "response": "Crypto (cryptocurrency) wani kudi ne na digital da ke amfani da blockchain technology. Ba a kula da shi da banki ba. Misalai: Bitcoin, USDT, da GDEG (na HARZ). Za ka iya siyarwa da saye, kuma zai iya zama saka hannun jari. Amma kula — farashin yana canzawa, don haka ka yi bincike kafin ka saka kuɾi."},
    {"instruction": "Ta yaya zan kula da lafiyar iyayata?", "response": "Don kula da lafiyar iyaye:\n1. Kai su asibiti don duba lafiya a kowace shekara\n2. Kula da abincin su — abinci mai gina jiki\n3. Tabbatar da suna shan magani idan suna da hawan jini ko ciwon sukari\n4. Kasancewa tare da su — rashin kwantar da hankali yana cutar da lafiya\n5. Kasancewa cikin kulawa game da motsa jiki\n6. Tabbbatar da suna barci lafiya\n7. Taimaka musu da aikin gida\n8. Saita emergency contact numbers a wayarsu"},
    {"instruction": "Yadda za ka rubuta CV?", "response": "Rubuta CV:\n1. Sunanka, wayar ka, email\n2. Takaitaccen bayani akan kanka\n3. Kwarewar aiki — kamfani, mukami, kwanakin\n4. Ilimi — makaranta, digiri, shekara\n5. Kwarewa — masu dacewa da aikin\n6. Kiyaye shafi 1-2 kadai\n7. Adana a matsayin PDF\n8. Yi amfani da kalmomin da ke cikin job description"},
    {"instruction": "Me zan yi don samun kuɗi a intanet?", "response": "Hanyoyin samun kuɗi a intanet:\n1. Siyar da digital products (ebooks, courses)\n2. Freelancing a Upwork, Fiverr\n3. Content creation a YouTube, TikTok\n4. Affiliate marketing\n5. Social media management\n6. Web design\n7. Online tutoring\nMuhimmin abu: koya dabara, ka yi amfani da ita, kuma ka ci gaba."},
    {"instruction": "Ta yaya zan koya coding?", "response": "Don koya coding:\n1. Fara da HTML/CSS — mai sauki\n2. Koya JavaScript — don interactivity\n3. Koya Python — mai amfani sosai\n4. Kullum ka yi aiki — mintuna 30 a kalla\n5. Ka gina projects — karami karami\n6. Koya a freeCodeCamp (kyauta)\n7. Kullum ka rubuta code, ba kallon kallon ba\n8. Shiga cikin coding communities\nHARZ tana da Python Programming book (N2,500) da Web Development book (N2,500)."},
    {"instruction": "Me ne AI (Artificial Intelligence)?", "response": "AI (Artificial Intelligence) wani tsarin ne na kwamfyuta da zai iya yin abubuwan da ake buƙatar hankalin mutum. Kamar ni — na HARZ AI! Na iya amsa tambayoyi, rubuta, fassara, da taimaka wa kasuwanci. An horar da ni akan bayanan HARZ Digital Services."},
    {"instruction": "Ta yaya zan yi safe intanet?", "response": "Don safe intanet:\n1. Yi amfani da password mai karfi — haruffa 12+\n2. Kunna two-factor authentication (2FA)\n3. Kar ka danna links masu shakka\n4. Kar ka bayyana OTP ga kowa\n5. Yi amfani da passwords daban-daban\n6. Kunna fingerprint lock a wayarka\n7. Kar ka amfani da WiFi na jama'a don banking\n8. Kula da phishing emails — duba adiresan mai aikawa"},
    {"instruction": "Yadda za ka fara gona a Najeriya?", "response": "Don fara gona a Najeriya:\n1. Kayi tunani akan abin da za ka noma (amfanin gona, dabbobi, kifi)\n2. Samu fili mai kyau\n3. Binciko kasuwar abin da za ka noma\n4. Fara karami — kada ka saka duk kuɗinka\n5. Kula da ruwa da taki\n6. Samu ilimi akan farming (HARZ tana da Agriculture ebook, N4,500)\n7. Binciko government loans da goyon baya\n8. Saita tsarin siyarwa kafin girbi\nFara karami, koya, sannan ka fadada."},
    {"instruction": "Me ya sa ya kamata in koya digital skills?", "response": "Dalilai na koya digital skills:\n1. Ayyuka na gaba suna buƙatar digital skills\n2. Za ka iya aiki a gida (remote work)\n3. Za ka iya samun kuɗi daga intanet\n4. Za ka fi samun aiki idan ka fi kowa kwarewa\n5. Za ka iya fara kasuwancinka\n6. Duniya tana canzawa — kada ka rage baya\n7. Za ka iya taimaka wa wasu da ilimin ka\n8. Digital skills ba su tsufa — suna kara daraja"},
    {"instruction": "Ta yaya zan guji scam a intanet?", "response": "Don guji scam a intanet:\n1. Kar ka aika kuɗi ga wanda ba ka san shi ba\n2. Kar ka amince da 'investment' da ke alkawari babban riba\n3. Kar ka bayyana OTP, password, ko BVN ga kowa\n4. Bank ba zai taba neman password ka ba\n5. Duba kafin ka sayi komai a intanet\n6. Yi amfani da verified platforms (Gumroad, Paystack)\n7. Kar ka danna links masu shakka a email ko WhatsApp\n8. Idan wani ya ce ka yi masa transfer kuma zai mayar maka da riba — zamba ce!"},
    {"instruction": "Yadda za ka yi amfani da WhatsApp don kasuwanci?", "response": "WhatsApp don kasuwanci:\n1. Saiti WhatsApp Business (kyauta)\n2. Saka catalog da products dinka\n3. Yi amfani da Status don talla kullum\n4. Ka kirkiro broadcast list don customers\n5. Saita auto-reply don tambayoyi\n6. Karɓi biyan kuɗi ta bank transfer\n7. Amsa saƙon cikin sauri\n8. Rabe customers da labels (new, paid, pending)\n9. Tura promo kullum amma kar ka yi yawa"},
